==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|mahhōn}}, from {{proto|Germanic|makōnan|lang=de}}; akin to Dutch {{term|maken}}, Old Saxon {{term|makan}}, Old English {{term|macian}} ( Modern English {{term|make}} ). Ultimately from {{proto|Indo-European|mag-|to knead, mix, make|lang=de}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈmaχn/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-machen.ogg|audio (Germany)}}
* {{audio|De-at-machen.ogg|audio (Austria)}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-weak|macht|machte|gemacht}}

# {{transitive|lang=de}} to [[do]], to [[make]] {{qualifier|to perform an action}}
#: '''''Mach''' es!'' — Do it!
#: ''Er hat eine große [[Schweinerei]] '''gemacht'''.'' — He has made a big mess.
#: ''Das hat er ganz allein '''gemacht'''!'' — He has done that all by himself!
# {{transitive|lang=de}} to [[make]]; to [[produce]]
#: ''Ich '''hab''' dir einen Kuchen '''gemacht'''!'' — I have made you a pie!
# {{transitive|lang=de}} to [[prepare]] {{qualifier|e.g. food, drinks}}
#: '''''Machst''' du heute das Essen?'' — Will you prepare dinner, today?
#: ''sich eine Pizza '''machen''''' — to prepare a pizza by oneself
# {{transitive|lang=de}} to [[matter]]
#: ''Das '''macht''' nichts!'' — That doesn't matter!
# {{transitive|informal|colloquial|lang=de}} to [[come to]]; to [[total]]; to [[cost]] {{qualifier|prizes}}
#: ''Wie viel '''macht''' das?'' — How much does that come to?
# {{transitive|informal|colloquial|lang=de}} to [[earn]]; to [[profit]] {{qualifier|wages}}
#: ''Der Herr Müller ist echt reich; der '''macht''' mehr als 5000 im Monat.'' — Mr Müller is quite rich; he earns more than 5000 bucks per month.

====Usage notes====
In opposite to English, the verbs {{term|machen|lang=de}} and {{term|tun|lang=de}} ( cognates of English {{term|make|lang=en}} and {{term|do|lang=en}}, respectively ) can be used synonymously in certain cases.

:If the verb implies an action, both ''machen'' and ''tun'' may be used interchangeable.
:: ''Ich bin mir nicht sicher, ob ich das '''tun'''/'''machen''' kann.'' — I am not sure if I can '''do''' that.
:: ''Wer hat sie umgebracht? Wer hat es '''getan'''?'' — Who has put her to death? Who has '''done''' it?

:But if there is something to be produced or made, ''machen'' ( like its cognate ''to make'' in English ) is preferred, instead.
:: ''Hast du den Kuchen '''gemacht'''? Er ist sehr lecker!.'' — Have you '''made''' the cake? It's delicious!. 

